{"builder":"Goal: add a --tsv flag to the CLI so it meets both of these acceptance lines:\n- \"node bin/csv2json.js --tsv reads tab-separated input on stdin and prints the same JSON array that node bin/csv2json.js prints for the same table as CSV\"\n- \"README.md documents --tsv\"\n\nContext: bin/csv2json.js reads stdin and prints JSON.stringify(parse(text)) followed by a newline. src/parse.js already exports parse(text, sep = ','), which splits on a separator you pass in. Tests are in test/csv.test.js and run with `npm test` (node --test).\n\nSteps:\n1. In bin/csv2json.js, check whether process.argv includes '--tsv'. If it does, call parse(text, '\\t'); if not, call parse(text) as it does now. Leave the default CSV behaviour and the output format (JSON plus a trailing newline) unchanged.\n2. In README.md, document the flag. Add a usage example such as `node bin/csv2json.js --tsv < table.tsv` and one sentence saying that --tsv reads tab-separated input instead of CSV. The literal text `--tsv` must appear in README.md.\n3. In test/csv.test.js, add a test that runs `node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` with input 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' and expects '[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]\\n'. That is the same output the existing CSV cli test expects.\n4. Run `npm test` and make sure every test passes. Then run this sanity check, which must print SAME:\n   csv=$(printf 'name,age,city\\nAda,36,London\\nBob,,Paris\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js); tsv=$(printf 'name\\tage\\tcity\\nAda\\t36\\tLondon\\nBob\\t\\tParis\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv); echo \"CSV: $csv\"; echo \"TSV: $tsv\"; [ \"$csv\" = \"$tsv\" ] && echo SAME || echo DIFFERENT\n5. Open one pull request that contains the code, README and test changes.","verifier":"Run every check from the repository root at the PR head.\n\nCheck 1. Expect line: \"node bin/csv2json.js --tsv reads tab-separated input on stdin and prints the same JSON array that node bin/csv2json.js prints for the same table as CSV\"\nRun:\n  csv=$(printf 'name,age,city\\nAda,36,London\\nBob,,Paris\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js); tsv=$(printf 'name\\tage\\tcity\\nAda\\t36\\tLondon\\nBob\\t\\tParis\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv); echo \"CSV: $csv\"; echo \"TSV: $tsv\"; [ \"$csv\" = \"$tsv\" ] && echo SAME || echo DIFFERENT\nPASS: both commands exit without error, the last line printed is SAME, and the CSV line is the JSON array [{\"name\":\"Ada\",\"age\":\"36\",\"city\":\"London\"},{\"name\":\"Bob\",\"age\":\"\",\"city\":\"Paris\"}]. That confirms the TSV output is that same array, with tabs split into columns.\nFAIL: either command errors, the last line is DIFFERENT, or the TSV output keeps tab characters inside its keys or values.\n\nCheck 2. Expect line: \"README.md documents --tsv\"\nRun:\n  grep -n -e '--tsv' README.md\nThen read the lines it reports.\nPASS: grep exits 0, and at least one matching line explains or shows that --tsv makes the CLI read tab-separated input (a usage example or a description counts).\nFAIL: grep finds nothing (exit 1), or the matches never say what the flag does.\n\nAdvisory (not part of PASS or FAIL):\n- Run `npm test`. All tests should pass, including the existing CSV tests. A new test that covers --tsv is good to have.\n- CSV output without the flag should be unchanged from before the PR."}